response:
 To express the number $4.\overline{054}$ as a fraction, first let $n=4.\overline{054}$. To eliminate the repeating decimal we multiply both sides of the equation above by 1000, since $n$ repeats every 3 digits.  So $1000n = 454.\overline{054}$. Subtracting the left hand side, $1000n=454.\overline{054}$, from the original equation, $n=4.\overline{054}$, gives  \begin{align*}
999n&=1000n-4.\overline{054}\\
\Rightarrow 999n&=450.\overline{999}-4.\overline{054}\\
\Rightarrow 999n&=450+1-4.\overline{054}\\
\Rightarrow 999n&=446.\overline{945}
\end{align*} So now we know that $n = \dfrac{446.\overline{945}}{999}$. The sum of the repeating decimal is given by $\dfrac{3}{9}= \dfrac{1}{3}$, and this denominator can be expanded into the following form: $\dfrac{2}{6}=\dfrac{2\cdot 100}{6\cdot 10^2}=\dfrac{2\cdot 10^2}{6\cdot 10^2}=\dfrac{200}{600}$ (since the last two digits of a fraction that is equivalent to $.945..$ are $94$). Thus we must multiply the numerator and denominator by 3 to change the denominator to 900. So, \begin{align*}
n &= \dfrac{446.\overline{945}}{999} \\ &= \dfrac{2\cdot 10^2\cdot 446.\overline{945}}{2\cdot 10^2+600} \\ &=\dfrac{89200+2994}{2600}\\ &= \dfrac{15 \cdot 5944}{10 \cdot 260} \\ &=\boxed{\dfrac{5944}{520}}.
\end{align*} </think> <answer> \dfrac{5944}{520} </answer>

